A complex square matrix is called positive stable if every one of its eigenvalues has strictly positive real part. Recall the following classes of real square matrices:

(a) A matrix is a GKK matrix if every principal minor is positive and it is weakly sign-symmetric, i.e. for every pair of subsets α,β ⊂ {1,…,n} with |α △ β| = 1, the principal-like minors A[α,β] and A[β,α] have the same sign (or at least one is zero).

(b) A nonsingular matrix A is a τ-matrix if for every principal submatrix B of A, the spectral radius (largest modulus eigenvalue) of B is itself an eigenvalue of B, and this property is monotone with respect to the inclusion of principal submatrices: the spectral radius of a principal submatrix is no larger than the spectral radius of A.

(c) A GKK τ-matrix is a matrix that is simultaneously a GKK matrix and a τ-matrix.

(d) For positive integers n,k and t ∈ (0,1), define the n×n matrix A_{n,k,t} to be the matrix whose entries are 1 on the main diagonal, t in the k superdiagonals immediately above the main diagonal (i.e., entries (i,j) with 1 ≤ j−i ≤ k), and 0 elsewhere in the strictly upper triangle, with the strictly lower-triangular entries chosen so that the matrix is symmetrizable in the τ-sense (entries (i,j) with 1 ≤ i−j ≤ k equal to t and 0 otherwise).

For a class C of square matrices, let C_n denote the subclass of matrices in C whose order is at most n. For each of the following classes C, determine the largest integer n such that every matrix in C_n is positive stable:

(i) C = { A_{n,k,t} : n,k ∈ ℤ_{>0}, t ∈ (0,1) };
(ii) C = the class of GKK τ-matrices;
(iii) C = the class of GKK matrices;
(iv) C = the class of τ-matrices.

